<ArticleLayout
  title="Build a small sampled instrument in Ableton Live without losing the source"
  description="A conservative Sampler workflow for pitch coverage, dynamics, edits, zones and round robins — designed to stay understandable after the first session."
  canonicalPath="/sound-design/sampled-instruments/building-a-sampled-instrument/"
  section="Sound Design"
  sectionPath="/sound-design/"
  readingTime="10 min read"
  method="This guide follows Ableton’s documented Sampler and file-management behavior. It does not claim that an unpublished viola instrument was recorded or provide invented audio demonstrations."
  sources={sources}
  related={related}
>
  <p>A playable sampled instrument begins before Sampler. If filenames, pitch reference and source takes are inconsistent, mapping only hides the confusion behind a keyboard.</p>
  <p>Start small: one articulation, a limited range, two or three dynamic levels and no more round robins than you can record consistently. A coherent twelve-note instrument teaches more than a half-finished four-octave library.</p>

  <h2>Define the instrument on one page</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>musical range and root notes to capture</li>
    <li>articulation and note length</li>
    <li>dynamic layers</li>
    <li>round robins per layer</li>
    <li>sample rate, bit depth and microphone position</li>
    <li>intended use and rights</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Record a tuning reference and room tone. Keep setup photographs only if they are real and useful; written distance and angle notes are still essential.</p>

  <h2>Choose roots by timbral change, not a fixed interval</h2>
  <p>Sampler can transpose a recording across several notes, but pitch shifting also moves attacks, resonances and noise. Capture more root notes where the instrument changes character or where transposition becomes obvious. Sparse mapping may be fine for a designed texture; an exposed melodic instrument needs closer roots.</p>

  <h2>Separate source, edit and export</h2>
  <p>Never destructively clean the only recording. Use three folders:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Source:</strong> full takes exactly as recorded.</li>
    <li><strong>Edits:</strong> selected regions with fades, tuning notes and repair history.</li>
    <li><strong>Instrument samples:</strong> consistently named files ready for mapping.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>A filename such as <code>vla_sus_C3_mf_rr02.wav</code> can be parsed by a person and, later, by a script. Document whether pitch names refer to sounding or written pitch.</p>

  <h2>Edit the attack before chasing a perfect loop</h2>
  <p>For bowed or evolving sounds, the attack determines whether repeated notes feel connected or mechanical. Place the start point consistently but do not cut away all pre-attack information. Use short fades to avoid clicks.</p>
  <p>Loop only where the sound genuinely supports it. Find a relatively stable region, match waveform direction and use a crossfade long enough to hide the seam without turning the centre of the note into a static blur. Some samples are better left unlooped.</p>

  <h2>Map one dimension at a time</h2>
  <p>Load the clean files into Sampler and set root notes first. Then define key zones. After pitch coverage plays correctly, add velocity zones. Only then introduce round robins or alternation logic. Debugging pitch, velocity and variation simultaneously makes mistakes hard to locate.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Question</th><th>Failure to watch</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Key zone</td><td>Which notes may this root cover?</td><td>Obvious time/pitch stretch at zone edges</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Velocity</td><td>Is this a timbre change or only volume?</td><td>Sudden tonal jump between layers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Round robin</td><td>Are repetitions genuinely different?</td><td>Uneven tuning or noise that sounds like a mistake</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Loop</td><td>Does the sustain remain alive?</td><td>Periodic pulsing or phasey crossfade</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Level-match before programming expression</h2>
  <p>Normalize only with a clear reason. Per-sample gain inside Sampler can balance layers while preserving the original files. Match perceived loudness across neighboring roots, then preserve the intentional dynamic relationship between soft and loud layers.</p>
  <p>Use modulation for behavior that belongs to the instrument — filter response, amplitude, subtle start variation — but do not process away the identity you recorded.</p>

  <h2>Package the playable instrument</h2>
  <p>Save the preset inside a dedicated Live Project and use Collect All and Save so referenced files travel with it. Ableton notes that plug-ins cannot be collected, so document any external dependency. Test the copied Project from a different folder before calling it portable.</p>
  <p>Keep a small test Set containing scales, repeated notes, velocity ramps and held notes. That file is the regression test when you change zones or replace samples.</p>
